WEBVTT

00:00:00.144 --> 00:00:04.417
Now you will write a short transaction using the earlier example as a pattern.

00:00:04.417 --> 00:00:11.872
The task is to change one patient age, inspect the changed row with RETURNING, then roll the transaction back.

00:00:11.872 --> 00:00:14.297
Start with the transaction boundary.

00:00:14.297 --> 00:00:18.807
Then write an UPDATE that targets the patient by the stable medical record number.

00:00:18.807 --> 00:00:22.342
Add RETURNING so you can inspect the temporary change.

00:00:22.342 --> 00:00:27.937
After that, undo the transaction and use a final SELECT to verify that the original value is back.

00:00:27.937 --> 00:00:30.487
Do not rush to copy a complete solution.

00:00:30.487 --> 00:00:35.422
Build the workflow one step at a time and run it when you think the sequence is correct.

00:00:35.422 --> 00:00:39.597
The goal is to understand the lifecycle: begin, change, inspect, undo, verify.

00:00:39.597 --> 00:00:42.202
Transactions let several statements behave as one logical unit.

00:00:42.202 --> 00:00:48.252
That matters when partial success would leave the database inconsistent—for example,

00:00:48.252 --> 00:00:53.982
creating an order header without its order lines or moving money out of one account without recording the

00:00:53.982 --> 00:00:58.016
corresponding destination. COMMIT confirms the unit; ROLLBACK abandons it.
